[
  {
    "claim": "Breach of OA §5.4 (affiliate transactions): Company payments to Castellan Asset Services LLC (100% Castellan-owned) — asset-management fee of $180,000/yr paid 2022-2026 (total $1,050,000) plus monthly 6% of gross rents — without the written disclosure to Members that §5.4 requires for transactions over $50,000/yr, and allegedly not on terms 'no less favorable' than arm's-length",
    "theory": "express_contract",
    "direct_or_derivative": "derivative (harm to the Company; recovery to the Company — the library expressly treats 'excessive fees paid to an affiliate' as derivative)",
    "survives_waiver": true,
    "limitations": {
      "accrual": "Each payment is a separate accrual: 2022-09-01, 2023-09-01, 2024-09-01, 2025-09-01, 2026-09-01 (the §5.4 disclosure was due within 30 days of each). The 2022-08-15 resolution engaging Castellan accrued then, so a challenge to the engagement itself expired 2025-08-15 absent tolling. 10 Del. C. §8106 window measured from today (2026-10-08) opens 2023-10-08.",
      "barred_portion": 375000,
      "tolling_argument": "Absent tolling, the 2022 ($180,000) and 2023 ($195,000) payments are outside the 3-year period = $375,000 of the $1,050,000 asset-management fees (35.7%). Tolling is available on two grounds recognized in the library: (a) inherently unknowable injury — the fees were paid by a Company the client does not manage and appear only in bank records the Manager produced on request in 2026; (b) fraudulent/concealment-based tolling — §5.4 imposed a contractual duty to disclose in writing within 30 days and no written disclosure is in the file, and the library states that breach of that §5.4-style disclosure duty supports concealment-based tolling as to the undisclosed fees. Equitable tolling on a fiduciary-reliance rationale is weak here because §7.3 eliminated fiduciary duties. Client must have acted promptly on discovery (she obtained records and a broker opinion in 2026 and should file within weeks). Amounts of the monthly 6%-of-gross-rents fees are not in the client file, so the barred portion of that component cannot be quantified."
    },
    "strength": "medium",
    "remedy": "Contract damages to the Company measured by the excess over an arm's-length fee, and/or restitution of payments made outside the §5.4 permission (the library does not state what remedy Delaware allows for breach of an LLC-agreement fee condition — label that as unverified). Practical value to the client is only her 18% share: 18% × $675,000 (in-window) = $121,500; 18% × $1,050,000 (if fully tolled) = $189,000. Demand futility is readily pleaded because the sole Manager is the interested party (§18-1001; Zuckerberg)."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Breach of OA §5.4 written-disclosure right personal to each Member (failure to disclose the Castellan engagement and each annual payment within 30 days)",
    "theory": "express_contract",
    "direct_or_derivative": "direct (the library treats breach of a contractual right personal to the member as direct; the disclosure ran 'to all Members')",
    "survives_waiver": true,
    "limitations": {
      "accrual": "30 days after each covered transaction/payment: 2022-10-01, 2023-10-01, 2024-10-01, 2025-10-01, 2026-10-01.",
      "barred_portion": 0,
      "tolling_argument": "Same concealment/inherently-unknowable argument as above. Note the irony that the breach of the disclosure duty is itself the best tolling argument, and that the earliest disclosure breaches (2022-10-01, 2023-10-01) sit just outside the 3-year window on 2026-10-08."
    },
    "strength": "low",
    "remedy": "Nominal-to-modest damages; its real value is (i) as the tolling predicate for the fee claims and (ii) as an undisputed contractual breach framing the equitable case. Damages for non-disclosure as such are not quantifiable from the file."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Breach of fiduciary duty (loyalty / self-dealing) against Bram Castellan in his capacity as MANAGER — the claim the client most wants",
    "theory": "fiduciary",
    "direct_or_derivative": "derivative as to the fees; direct as to the dilution",
    "survives_waiver": false,
    "limitations": {
      "accrual": "Same dates as the fee and dilution conduct (2022-08-15 / payment dates; 2026-08-20 to 2026-09-25).",
      "barred_portion": 0,
      "tolling_argument": "Moot — the claim fails at the waiver, not the limitations line. Note that the elimination of fiduciary duties also weakens fiduciary-rationale equitable tolling."
    },
    "strength": "low",
    "remedy": "None. OA §7.3 eliminates the Manager's duties of care and loyalty 'to the fullest extent permitted by the Delaware Limited Liability Company Act,' and 6 Del. C. §18-1101(c) permits elimination of fiduciary duties with maximum effect to freedom of contract. Do not lead with this claim; it invites a motion to dismiss and a fee award risk."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Breach of fiduciary duty against Bram Castellan in his capacity as an 82% CONTROLLING MEMBER (not as Manager) — for causing the capital call, dilution and book-value buyout to be used to acquire the client's interest at a fraction of value",
    "theory": "fiduciary",
    "direct_or_derivative": "direct (wrongful dilution of her own interest is direct per the library)",
    "survives_waiver": true,
    "limitations": {
      "accrual": "2026-08-20 (call notice), 2026-09-25 (dilution notice), 2026-10-01 (buyout offer) — comfortably inside 3 years.",
      "barred_portion": 0,
      "tolling_argument": "Not needed for the dilution conduct."
    },
    "strength": "medium",
    "remedy": "Rescission/declaration that the Dilution Event is void, damages measured by the value of the interest taken. CONDITIONAL, and this is the single most important open item: the excerpted §7.3 by its terms eliminates the duties of 'the Manager' only and says nothing about a Member's duties, and 6 Del. C. §18-1101(c) eliminates duties only 'by provisions in the limited liability company agreement.' If the complete agreement contains a member-level waiver, this claim disappears. We have only excerpts (see QUESTIONS.md). Also unverified: whether Delaware enforces such a capacity-limited waiver strictly — that proposition is not in the library."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Invalid Dilution Event — failure to satisfy OA §6.3 notice mechanics: the 2026-08-20 call was sent by text message with a PDF; no certified mail was sent; the email bounced to an old address on file. §6.3 requires written notice 'delivered to each Member by certified mail and email'",
    "theory": "express_contract",
    "direct_or_derivative": "direct (wrongful dilution of the member's own interest)",
    "survives_waiver": true,
    "limitations": {
      "accrual": "2026-08-20 (defective notice) / 2026-09-25 (notice of Dilution Event). Not time-barred.",
      "barred_portion": 0,
      "tolling_argument": "None needed. Note laches: in Chancery the 3-year period applies by analogy and delay in equitable claims is itself a risk — file promptly."
    },
    "strength": "high",
    "remedy": "Declaratory judgment that no Dilution Event occurred and the client remains an 18% Member; injunction against the Company exercising the §9.2 buyout option, which arises only 'following a Dilution Event.' The library rule is strict: the issuer must strictly comply with the agreement's notice mechanics, and a call that fails the specified delivery method does not trigger dilution. Timing was NOT the defect (2026-08-20 to 2026-09-20 is 31 days, which satisfies 'at least 30 days'); the delivery method is. Risks: the unexcerpted agreement may contain a notice section permitting other delivery methods or deeming notice effective when sent, and the client plainly received actual notice."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Invalid Dilution Event — the call was not 'for a bona fide Company purpose set forth in the call notice': the notice stated only '$1,500,000 additional capital for working capital and opportunities', and Bram funded the entire $1,500,000 including the client's $270,000, immediately diluting her and offering book value",
    "theory": "express_contract",
    "direct_or_derivative": "direct",
    "survives_waiver": true,
    "limitations": {
      "accrual": "2026-08-20 / 2026-09-25. Not time-barred.",
      "barred_portion": 0,
      "tolling_argument": "None needed."
    },
    "strength": "medium",
    "remedy": "Same relief as the notice-mechanics claim. Weaker than the delivery defect because 'working capital' is on its face a purpose; the claim needs evidence of pretext — what the $1,500,000 was actually spent on, and whether it was needed at all (see QUESTIONS.md). The library requires strict compliance with purpose requirements and treats a call lacking the stated bona fide purpose as not triggering dilution."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Implied covenant of good faith and fair dealing — the §6.3 'fair value of the Company as determined by the Manager in good faith' was exercised arbitrarily/in bad faith: $7,200,000 against a 2026-10-05 independent indication of $14,500,000 enterprise value / $9,800,000 equity value, and the resulting 6.1% cannot be reproduced from any pro rata formula using $7,200,000",
    "theory": "implied_covenant",
    "direct_or_derivative": "direct",
    "survives_waiver": true,
    "limitations": {
      "accrual": "2026-09-25. Not time-barred.",
      "barred_portion": 0,
      "tolling_argument": "None needed."
    },
    "strength": "high",
    "remedy": "Void the recomputation and order a good-faith determination; damages measured by her interest at the correct value (15.61% on a $9,800,000 equity base = $1,529,841 of value, versus 6.1%). This claim survives by design: §7.3 expressly preserves the implied covenant, 6 Del. C. §18-1101(c) forbids its elimination, and §18-1101(e) forbids limiting liability for a bad-faith violation of it. The library states a manager's 'good faith' fair-value determination is reviewable for arbitrariness or bad faith where the value is far below a contemporaneous independent indication — $7,200,000 is 26.53% below the independent equity indication and 50.34% below the independent enterprise value."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Implied covenant — using the capital-call → dilution → book-value-buyout machinery to strip an 18% Member for $189,100, i.e. 10.72% of the independent indication of the value of that interest, while the Manager's wholly owned affiliate collected $1,050,000 in undisclosed fees",
    "theory": "implied_covenant",
    "direct_or_derivative": "direct",
    "survives_waiver": true,
    "limitations": {
      "accrual": "2026-08-20 to 2026-10-01. Not time-barred.",
      "barred_portion": 0,
      "tolling_argument": "None needed."
    },
    "strength": "medium",
    "remedy": "Declaration plus damages; the library's Dieckman line supports policing a contractual process used deceptively to deprive a minority holder of the fruits of the bargain, notwithstanding exculpation. Risk: the covenant is only a gap-filler and cannot override express terms, so this theory must be tied to the discretion §6.3 confers (purpose, delivery, fair value) rather than to the fairness of book value itself."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Attack on the §9.2 buyout price standard itself (Book Value from the most recent year-end balance sheet = $3,100,000, versus $9,800,000 independent equity value — a $6,700,000 gap)",
    "theory": "express_contract",
    "direct_or_derivative": "direct",
    "survives_waiver": true,
    "limitations": {
      "accrual": "2026-10-01 (offer). Not time-barred.",
      "barred_portion": 0,
      "tolling_argument": "None needed."
    },
    "strength": "low",
    "remedy": "None on its own. The library is explicit that the implied covenant cannot override express terms and dilution/valuation provisions are enforced as written; a bargained-for book-value formula is exactly such a term. The correct attack is on the CONDITION PRECEDENT — no valid Dilution Event, therefore no §9.2 option — not on the price formula. Do not plead 'book value is unfair' as a standalone claim."
  },
  {
    "claim": "Declaratory relief on the characterization of the $270,000 Bram funded 'and Imani's share': Company capital contribution by Bram, a member loan to Imani, or an unauthorized advance",
    "theory": "other",
    "direct_or_derivative": "direct",
    "survives_waiver": true,
    "limitations": {
      "accrual": "2026-09-20 (funding date) / 2026-09-25. Not time-barred.",
      "barred_portion": 0,
      "tolling_argument": "None needed."
    },
    "strength": "medium",
    "remedy": "Outcome-determinative and unresolved on the file: if the $270,000 was an advance/loan to her, she keeps 18% but owes $270,000 (plus any agreed interest); if it was a Bram capital contribution, a valid dilution would move her to about 15.61% (fair-value-weighted) or 14.90% (at the Manager's own $7,200,000). The Company's ledger treatment must be obtained (QUESTIONS.md)."
  }
]

